<content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>We will solve these two problems as a class.</para>
<br/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item>Do these terms have the same variables with the same exponents? Explain your answer. <br/>
        <br/>
        \(6x^4y^2\)         \(8x^4y^2\) </item>
    </list>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Yes. Both terms have the same variables raised to the same degrees: \(x\) is raised to the fourth power, and \(y\) is squared.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><br/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
      <item> Add the terms together: \(6x^4y^2+8x^4y^2\). What is the sum? <br/>
      </item>
    </list>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(14x^4y^2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><br/>
<para>In problems 3 - 7, simplify the monomial addition and subtraction problems on your own. Write down your answer, then select the solution button to compare your work.</para>
